  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1247 AM CDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\nA broad H5 trough over the Ohio Valley and a sfc boundary in \nthe central Plains will set the stage for a return of unsettled \nweather today through Saturday. As a series of shortwaves round \na 597 dm ridge located over the southwest Rocky Mountains, \nintervals of scattered showers and thunderstorms will be \npossible, with rain chances peaking around 40-60% each day. The\nfocus this morning will be across portions of southeast \nMissouri and southern Illinois as a decaying complex of storms \nmoves through with embedded rumbles of thunder. Instability is \nmodest at best, favoring heavy downpours and lightning being the\nmain concern with storms. Sporadic rain chances shift more east\nFriday into Friday night before tapering off Saturday morning. \n\nThe aformentioned ridge builds more leeward of the Rockies Sunday \ninto Monday allowing for drier conditions across the FA. Southerly \nreturn flow will cause an influx of higher dewpoints in the 70s\nas the heat and humidity builds. High temperatures by the NBM \nare progged in the lower 90s with heat index values of 100 to \n105 degrees. However, the ridge quickly breaks down by the \nmiddle of next week, allowing for daily showers and \nthunderstorms to return. The height falls will likely put an \nend to the warming trend with temperatures eventually returning \nback to the 80s for the latter half of next week.",
